text:
Sanjay: quick sync — where should gradient/backoff live: operator hints or scaler final admission? short opinions please.

Julia: operator-side makes sense for quick telemetry-driven heuristics. controller already sees prewarm and cluster signals.

Kevin: downside: operator releases are slower. prefer a small hint contract so scaler can iterate without operator rollouts.

Maria: product teams want per-tenant overrides on the scaler; owner teams will expect opt-in controls.

Sanjay: compromise: operator emits HeadroomHint + confidence + backoff params; scaler keeps authority over tenant quotas and emits AdmissionVerdict. small contract over etcd/gRPC.

Kevin: contract fields I want: pool_id, headroom_gpus, confidence (0-1), expires_at, backoff_kind, max_rate, source_version.

Julia: how do we resolve conflicts? example: operator suggests reduce headroom but tenant has reserved SLA credits.

Maria: negotiation rule: scaler honors tenant-config (priority). operator can set cluster-safety flags (eviction_safety_threshold) that scaler must not violate. encode both in proto.

Sanjay: proto sketch in chat copy:
```
message HeadroomHint {
  string pool_id = 1;
  int32 headroom_gpus = 2;
  float confidence = 3; // 0-1
  int64 expires_at = 4;
  enum BackoffKind { LINEAR = 0; EXPONENTIAL = 1; }
  BackoffKind kind = 5;
  float backoff_param = 6;
  string source_version = 7;
}
message AdmissionVerdict {
  string pool_id = 1;
  enum Verdict { ALLOW = 0; THROTTLE = 1; REJECT = 2; }
  Verdict decision = 2;
  string reason = 3;
}
message BillingSignal {
  string pool_id = 1;
  int32 old_headroom = 2;
  int32 new_headroom = 3;
  string reason = 4;
  int64 timestamp = 5;
}
```

Kevin: TTL on hints + source_version is useful for debugging and canary. also recommend a small backoff curve enum and param.

Maria: observability: emit hint_emitted, hint_applied, hint_conflict metrics. dashboard should show applied_ratio, avg_delta, and conflict examples per-tenant. add spans for handoff path.

Julia: rollout: opt-in per pool. passive mode (log-only) => compare what scaler would do vs operator hint; then enforced per-pool after 48–72h pilot.

Sanjay: safety: if operator unreachable, scaler uses last valid hint until TTL then falls back to conservative static headroom.

ops-bot: deploy queued -> operator/v2 canary to staging :rocket:

Kevin: governance Q: can operator trigger global emergency throttle? that's risky.

Maria: emergency throttle allowed only if pool config has emergency_control enabled + 2 approvers and audit-log webhook. no silent global kills.

Julia: Console UX: show active hint, confidence, ttl, and final actor (scaler vs operator). toggles: prefer_operator_hint, scaler_enforce, deny_operator. Keep it simple for SRE.

Sanjay: billing: headroom changes mid-billing need a BillingSignal webhook with old/new headroom and reason for accounting. loop in billing.

Kevin: add billing_signal emission when scaler enacts a capacity-affecting decision.

Maria: metrics to add: hint_applied_ratio, avg_headroom_change, conflicts_per_hour, emergency_throttles_count. log conflict examples for SRE review.

Julia: pilot suggestion: pick a low-criticality pool with mixed tenants. passive mode 72h then enforced 48h with tight monitors.

Sanjay: next steps: Kevin+Julia draft proto & RFC; Maria builds dashboard skeleton; I'll schedule pilot with infra. Kevin to include rollback steps.

Kevin: will open a short RFC summarizing contract, conflict rules, emergency gating, metrics, billing events, and rollback.

Maria: follow-up question: how do prewarm tokens interact with headroom changes? need meter/billing clarity. add to RFC and loop in billing/metering.

Sanjay: agreed. pilot kickoff planned for 1764211800. keep opt-in and narrow to minimize blast radius. :thumbsup:
